# Deepcore — The mine, its bands, its tiles, and the surface

This file defines the world the game plays in: the tile grid and its coordinate
space, the camera over it, the world sizes, the four depth bands and the Core
chamber, every tile kind, what generation places where, and the surface camp.
Every figure below carries the name this specification gives it.

## The tile grid

The world is a grid of square tiles `TILE` (`80`) logical units on a side. A cell
is addressed by `col` and `row`, and the cell `(col, row)` occupies the world-space
rectangle `x` in `[col * TILE, col * TILE + TILE]`, `y` in
`[row * TILE, row * TILE + TILE]`.

| Figure | Name | Value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Grid width in columns | `WORLD_COLS` | `32` |
| World width in units | `WORLD_W` (`WORLD_COLS * TILE`) | `2560` |
| Playable columns | — | `1` through `30` |
| Border columns | — | `0` and `31` |
| Deepest row, at the Standard size | `STANDARD_ROWS` | `500` |
| Meters of depth per row | `METERS_PER_ROW` | `5` |
| World `y` of the surface ground line | `SURFACE_Y` (`TILE`) | `80` |

`Row 0` is the surface: open sky and camp ground, where the buildings stand and
the miner spawns. `Row 1` is the first row of ground. The deepest row is
`coreRow`, the Core chamber, set by the world size below; rows `1` through
`coreRow - 1` are minable. Depth is reported to the player in meters, and a miner
whose feet rest at world `y` is at `max(0, (y - SURFACE_Y) / TILE * METERS_PER_ROW)`
meters, so the top of `row r` is at `METERS_PER_ROW * (r - 1)` meters.

The grid is `WORLD_W` (`2560`) units wide and the viewport is `VIEW_W` (`1280`)
wide, so about half the mine's width is on screen at once and the world scrolls
horizontally as well as vertically.

## The camera

The camera is named by `camX` and `camY`, the world-space point drawn at the
top-left corner of the mine viewport.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "structured-2d")}}
The view is the engine's camera, positioned by the game each frame. It is left at
a zoom of `1` and no rotation, so a world unit and a logical unit are the same
length, and it is placed so that `(camX, camY)` falls on the viewport's top-left
corner.
{{else}}
The view is the game's own. A world position `(wx, wy)` is drawn at the viewport
position `(wx - camX, wy - camY + HUD_H)`.
{{/if}}

The camera follows the miner in both axes. Let `mx` and `my` be the miner's
center in world space and `vy` its vertical velocity.

- Horizontally the miner is centered:
  `camX = clamp(mx - VIEW_W / 2, 0, WORLD_W - VIEW_W)`.
- Vertically the camera leads the miner's travel:
  `camY = clamp(my - VIEW_H / 2 + lead, -Infinity, (coreRow + 1) * TILE - VIEW_H)`.

`lead` is a signed distance in units, positive downward, carried across frames and
starting at `0`. Each update it moves toward `leadTarget` at a rate that depends
on which way it is moving:

| Quantity | Name | Value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Full lead | `CAM_LEAD_MAX` | `212` |
| Seconds of sustained travel to reach full lead | `CAM_LEAD_RAMP` | `2` |
| Rate multiplier while `lead` moves toward `0` | `CAM_UNWIND_MULT` | `4` |
| Speed below which the miner counts as still | `CAM_STILL_SPEED` | `40` |

- `leadTarget` is `0` while `abs(vy) <= CAM_STILL_SPEED`, and otherwise
  `sign(vy) * CAM_LEAD_MAX`.
- `lead` moves toward `leadTarget` at `CAM_LEAD_MAX / CAM_LEAD_RAMP` units per
  second while the move takes it away from `0`, and at
  `CAM_UNWIND_MULT` times that rate while the move takes it toward `0`. It never
  overshoots `leadTarget` within an update.

A sustained descent therefore rides the miner up the view until its leading edge
sits about one tile from the top of the viewport, so the floor of a shaft or a
lava seam comes into view early; a sustained climb rides it symmetrically down. A
brief hop barely leads, and boring straight down at near-zero velocity holds the
miner at the center. The lead builds toward its target at a rate driven by time, not by speed,
so a slow drift and a fast plunge reach full lead over the same two seconds.

There is no ceiling above the surface, so the camera has no upper clamp and
follows the miner up into the open sky.

## World size

The player chooses a world size before the expedition starts. The size scales
only how deep the mine goes. The bands, the hazards, the ore mix, the economy,
the upgrades, and the rocket are identical at every size.

| Size | `WORLD_SIZE_SCALE` | `coreRow` | Core depth |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `quick` | `0.5` | `250` | `1250` m |
| `standard` | `1` | `500` | `2500` m |
| `marathon` | `2` | `1000` | `5000` m |

`coreRow` is `round(STANDARD_ROWS * WORLD_SIZE_SCALE)`. Everything else in this
specification that varies with depth is expressed as a depth fraction rather than
a row, so it is identical in shape at every size:

`depthFraction(row) = (row - 1) / (coreRow - 1)`, which runs `0` at `row 1` to `1`
at the deepest minable row.

The chosen size is part of the expedition, so it is carried in the save and reused
when an expedition is replayed.

## The four depth bands and the Core chamber

The minable rows are divided into four equal bands. The band index of a minable
row is `min(3, floor(4 * depthFraction(row)))`, so at the Standard size:

| Band | Index | Rows (Standard) | `BAND_HEALTH` | Hazards | Material | Gemstone |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Topsoil | `0` | `1`–`125` | `4` | none | — | none |
| Rockbed | `1` | `126`–`250` | `8` | gas | Resonite | Verdite |
| Deepstone | `2` | `251`–`375` | `12` | gas, lava | Cryenite | Roselite |
| Coreshell | `3` | `376`–`499` | `16` | gas, lava | — | Aurite |

`BAND_HEALTH` is the health of every minable tile in the band, which the drill
removes in damage-per-hit chunks, so a deeper band takes more hits, and so more
time and more fuel, to break.

A cell carries the band it was made with rather than reading it off its row each
time. Generation gives every cell the band its row falls in, so the two agree
throughout a generated mine. Where the mine's depth changes under cells that are
already in the ground, which `specs/instrumentation.md` describes, the cells that
come through keep the band they were made with, and their health and full health
follow that band rather than the row they now sit at.

The Core chamber is `row coreRow`. Every cell of it is bedrock border except the
Core tile, which sits at `CORE_COL` (`16`).

## Tile kinds

A cell's kind is fixed when the world is generated, except that a minable cell
becomes an empty tunnel once it is drilled out.

| Kind | Minable | Passable | Holds |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `bedrock` | no | no | Columns `0` and `31`, and the Core chamber row apart from the Core tile. |
| `rock` | yes | no | The plain rock of the row's band. Yields nothing. |
| `ore` | yes | no | One unit of a named ore or gemstone, banked in cargo when the tile breaks. |
| `material` | yes | no | One exotic material, Resonite or Cryenite, banked in the satchel when the tile breaks. |
| `gas` | yes | no | A gas pocket. Breaking it detonates instead of clearing cleanly. |
| `lava` | yes | no | Molten rock. Contact and drilling through both cost hull. |
| `stone` | no | no | An unbreakable boulder inside the playable field. |
| `core` | no | no | The Core, at `(CORE_COL, coreRow)`. Drilling downward onto it extracts a Core Sample and leaves the tile in place. |
| `tunnel` | — | yes | Open space, either carved or original. |

Every minable kind carries a health, starting at its band's `BAND_HEALTH` and
falling as the drill hits it. Health persists on the cell: a partly drilled tile
keeps its remaining health when the miner leaves and resumes from there.

Unbreakable stone sits inside the playable field rather than at its border, so a
vertical shaft that meets one has to jog sideways past it. No drill breaks it, but
the explosives field supplies clear it.

A cell may also carry a ground item resting on it. A jettisoned Core Sample is the
only one. A ground item is not a tile kind and rides on top of an open tunnel.

## What generation places

The mine is generated fresh for each expedition from the current seed, within the
rules below. There is no fixed map.

- Every cell of columns `1`–`30`, rows `1` through `coreRow - 1`, is minable
  unless a rule below makes it something else.
- `(CAVE_MOUTH_COL, 1)` (`26`, `1`) is an open tunnel: the way down out of the
  camp.
- Ore veins occupy `ORE_DENSITY` (`0.14`) of the minable cells, the same share at
  every depth, so the share of rock that is ore never spikes in one stratum. No
  ore appears above `ORE_MIN_ROW` (`4`), so the first three rows of ground are
  plain rock.
- Unbreakable stone occupies a share of the minable cells that rises linearly with
  `depthFraction` from `STONE_DENSITY_MIN` (`0.02`) at the top of the rockbed to
  `STONE_DENSITY_MAX` (`0.08`) at the bottom of the coreshell, and none appears in
  the topsoil.
- Gas pockets occupy a share rising linearly from `GAS_DENSITY_MIN` (`0.004`) at
  the top of the rockbed to `GAS_DENSITY_MAX` (`0.012`) at the bottom of the
  coreshell, and none appears in the topsoil.
- Lava occupies a share rising linearly from `LAVA_DENSITY_MIN` (`0.03`) at the
  top of the deepstone to `LAVA_DENSITY_MAX` (`0.10`) at the bottom of the
  coreshell, and none appears above the deepstone. Lava cells cluster into pools
  rather than scattering as single cells.
- Exactly one Resonite node exists, at a random minable cell of the rockbed band,
  and exactly one Cryenite node exists, at a random minable cell of the deepstone
  band. Neither sits at a fixed cell, and both are always present.

Two properties hold in every generated mine, at every size and every seed:

- From the cave mouth there is a path to each material node and to the Core that
  crosses only minable cells that are neither lava nor unbreakable stone, so a
  route down is always diggable without drilling lava or blasting a boulder.
- No band is sealed across its full width by lava or unbreakable stone.

Every measurable share above holds within `DENSITY_TOLERANCE` (`0.25`) of the
stated value, measured relative to that value over a whole band.

## The surface

`Row 0` is the camp: scrapped ground under a dim sky, open space the miner walks
and hovers across. Nothing is drilled up here, and there is no ceiling above it.

Six buildings stand in the camp. The miner activates one by standing at it, and
each opens an overlay panel except the Save Pad, which saves on the spot.

| Building | Id | Does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Fuel Depot | `fuel-depot` | Buys jetpack fuel and hull repair for Credits. |
| Ore Market | `ore-market` | Sells the whole cargo for Credits. |
| Save Pad | `save-pad` | Saves the expedition to its single slot. No panel. |
| Upgrade Shop | `upgrade-shop` | Buys the next tier on any of the seven upgrade tracks. |
| Supply Depot | `supply-depot` | Buys the six single-use field supplies. |
| Launch Pad | `launch-pad` | Fabricates the next rocket component, and launches. |

Each building's footprint is a rectangle in world space. Where the six sit along
the camp is yours, subject to all of the following:

- Every footprint's base sits on the ground line: `y + h` equals `SURFACE_Y`
  (`80`), and the building rises from there into the open sky.
- Every footprint lies within columns `1`–`30` and covers no part of
  `(CAVE_MOUTH_COL, 1)`.
- No two footprints overlap, and any two footprints are separated horizontally by
  at least `BUILDING_GAP` (`40`) units of clear ground.
